 61TI c  {+61}Sc nuclide has been identified but no information about its       
 61TI2c  decay to {+61}Ti is available                                          
 61TI c  1997Be70: {+61}Ti identified in projectile fission of a 750 MeV/nucleon
 61TI2c  {+238}U beam incident on a Be target, followed by fragment separation  
 61TI3c  using FRS at GSI facility. Measured production cross section.          
 61TI4c  Nine events were assigned to {+61}Ti with corresponding cross section  
 61TI5c  of 2.5 nb.                                                             
 61TI c  2011Da08 (also 2002MaZN thesis): {+61}Ti produced in the fragmentation 
 61TI2c  of 57.8 MeV/nucleon {+86}Kr beam impinged on 50 mg/cm{+2} thick        
 61TI3c  tantalum target using LISE-2000 spectrometer at GANIL facility.        
 61TI4c  Detector system included a three-element Si-detector telescope         
 61TI5c  containing a double-sided silicon-strip detector (DSSSD) backed by a   
 61TI6c  Si(Li) detector and surrounded by four clover type EXOGAM Ge detectors.
 61TI7c  Product identified by mass, atomic number, charge, energy loss and     
 61TI8c  time-of-flight methods. Measured half-life.                            
 61TI c  2019Wi04: two nanosecond isomers and gamma rays identified in this     
 61TI2c  work from {+9}Be({+238}U,X) reaction at RIBF-RIKEN.                    
 61TI c  A very weak |g ray at 126 {I2} reported by 2002MaZN was seen           
 61TI2c  in coincidence with heavy ions. This may either be an isomeric         
 61TI3c  transition in {+61}Ti or a |g ray in {+61}V emitted by the             
 61TI4c  decay of {+61}Ti. But a 127 {I2} |g has also been reported by          
 61TI5c  2005Ga01 in the decay of {+61}V to {+61}Cr. The 126|g seen by          
 61TI6c  2002MaZN is probably the same as the 125.0|g by 2019Wi04               
 61TI c  Theoretical calculations: 1995Ri05 Shell model calculations:           
 61TI2c  predicted spin, binding energy, and mass defect.                       
 61TI c  Theory references: consult the NSR database (www.nndc.bnl.gov/nsr/)    
 61TI2c  for four references for structure calculations.                        
